WHAT WAS YOUR SPECIFIC ROLE IN THE WOMEN SAFETY APP

In the Women Safety App, RakshaNet, my main contribution was working on the concept and development of the safety solution and helping translate the real-world problem into practical application features.

The core idea of the project was to provide a quick way for a user to request help during an emergency. We focused on features such as a one-tap SOS mechanism and location sharing so that emergency information could be communicated quickly.

I was also involved in exploring the application design and the technologies required for the solution. Firebase was used for backend-related services, while map functionality was integrated to support location visualization.

One of the most challenging ideas we explored was providing emergency support in situations with poor or limited internet connectivity. This led us to explore mesh networking and peer-to-peer emergency communication concepts.

My biggest contribution and learning from the project was understanding how to move from identifying a social problem to designing a practical technical solution. It also helped me understand the importance of user experience, emergency workflows, location services, backend integration, and the limitations of real-world connectivity.

Since safety applications are sensitive and real-world reliability is extremely important, I would also clearly mention the current limitations of the prototype and the areas that would require further testing and development before considering it a production-ready safety solution.







TELL ME ABOUT A TIME A TECHNICAL PROJECT FAILED OR MISSED A DEADLINE

One situation that taught me an important lesson was during a project where we had planned multiple features and initially underestimated the time required for integrating and testing everything.

We were focused on adding several features, but as development progressed, we faced technical issues and integration took longer than we had expected. Because of that, we had to revise our timeline and prioritize the most important functionality instead of trying to complete everything at once.

My approach was first to identify what was actually blocking progress rather than simply continuing to add more features. We divided the remaining work into smaller tasks, prioritized the core functionality, and focused on getting the most important features working properly.

I also learned the importance of communicating early when there is a risk of delay. Instead of hiding the problem, it is better to explain the technical issue, discuss the available options, and create a realistic plan.

The main lesson I learned was that project planning should include time for debugging, integration, and testing, not only for writing code. Since then, I have tried to break projects into smaller milestones and regularly check progress against the planned timeline.









